cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "वाक् (Vāk): a Sanskrit-native programming language with a kāraka type system"
message: "If you use Vāk in your research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Vidyadheesh
    family-names: Pandurangi
    email: vidyadheeshp@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/vidyadheeshp/vak"
url: "https://vidyadheeshp.github.io/vak/"
abstract: >-
  Vāk is a programming language whose keywords are Sanskrit and whose type
  system is Pāṇini's kāraka analysis: a parameter declares the grammatical role
  it plays in the action — agent, patient, instrument, source — and the analyser
  enforces the grammar of those roles, including Pāṇini's rule that an action
  has at most one agent and one patient. Because the roles are marked, argument
  order is deliberately unconstrained, which is the same freedom case endings
  give a Sanskrit sentence. Every stage of the front end — lexer, parser,
  semantic analyser, compiler and virtual machine — is written in Vāk itself,
  and five independent execution engines are held to byte-identical output.
keywords:
  - programming language
  - Sanskrit
  - Panini
  - karaka
  - type system
  - self-hosting compiler
  - Devanagari
license: MIT
version: 0.11.1
